body:
```suggestion
	enum {
		ANIMATION_NODE_NOTIFICATION_STATE_MACHINE_STARTED,
		ANIMATION_NODE_NOTIFICATION_STATE_MACHINE_FINISHED
	};
```
Managing IDs with sequential numbers across classes is not recommended. Although it is fine to name the enum with the name of the Node like `ANIMATION_NODE_NOTIFICATION_[CLASS NAME]_[SIGNAL NAME]`.

If you really want to do this, at least for example, have the ID for each AnimationNode in an enum on the AnimationTree side, and multiply it by 1000 to add it so that the AnimationNode can have signals from 0 to 999 like:

```
enum {
	ANIMATION_NODE_NOTIFICATION_STATE_MACHINE_STARTED = AnimationNode::ANIMATION_NODE_NOTIFICATION_STATE_MACHINE * AnimationNode::SIGNAL_MAX + 0,
	ANIMATION_NODE_NOTIFICATION_STATE_MACHINE_FINISHED = AnimationNode::ANIMATION_NODE_NOTIFICATION_STATE_MACHINE * AnimationNode::SIGNAL_MAX + 1,
};
```

However, I think it is still complicated, so I think it is easiest for the users to understand by passing class names to the signals. As long as class_name is passed to the signal as StringName, I think there will be no performance problems due to string comparisons unless the user makes a mistake.

